.TH uniq "" "Command"
.SH NAME
uniq \- remove/count repeated lines in a sorted file
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBuniq [\-cdu] [\-\fIn\fB] [+\fIn\fB] [\fIinfile\fB [\fIoutfile\fB]]\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
uniq -- remove/count repeated lines in a sorted file
Usage:	uniq [ options ] [ infile [ outfile] ]
Options:
	-c	Print duplication count with lines
	-d	Print only duplicated lines
	-n	Skip 'n' fields during comparison
	+n	Skip 'n' characters (after skipping fields)
	-u	Print only non-repeated lines
.HE
.B uniq
reads
.I infile
(or the standard input, if
.I infile
is omitted) and copies it to
.I outfile
(or the standard output, if
.I outfile
is omitted), collapsing each run of two or more equal, adjacent lines
into (by default) a single copy.
Because
.B uniq
only compares each line against the one before it,
.I infile
should already be sorted.
.IP \fB\-c\fR
Precede each output line with the number of times it occurred in that
run.
.IP \fB\-d\fR
Print only lines that occurred more than once, and only one copy of
each.
.IP \fB\-u\fR
Print only lines that occurred exactly once.
.PP
If none of
.BR \-c ,
.BR \-d ,
or
.B \-u
is given,
.B uniq
behaves as if both
.B \-d
and
.B \-u
were given, so every distinct line is printed once regardless of how
many times it was repeated.
.PP
Two further options control how much of each line is skipped before
lines are compared for equality (the full line is still copied to the
output):
.IP \fB\-\fIn\fR
Skip the first
.I n
fields of each line, where a field is a run of non-blank, non-tab
characters bounded by blanks or tabs.
.IP \fB+\fIn\fR
Skip the first
.I n
characters of each line, applied after any field-skipping requested by
\fB\-\fIn\fR.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR comm ,
.B sort
